<!-- 4-LAYER DETECTION ENGINE -->
<section class="vx-section" aria-labelledby="engine-title">
  <div class="vx-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Detection Engine</div>
    <h2 id="engine-title">4 layers of behavioral analysis</h2>
    <p>Traditional scanners check vulnerability databases. vexes goes deeper — it analyzes actual code, dependency graphs, version history, and registry metadata to catch attacks that no database has seen yet.</p>
    <div class="vx-features-grid" role="list">
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 1 — AST Code Analysis</h3>
        <p>Parses JavaScript and Python source via acorn AST. Detects <code>eval()</code>, <code>child_process.exec()</code>, credential harvesting, obfuscated code, WebAssembly payloads, DNS exfiltration, and prototype chain escapes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 2 — Dependency Graph</h3>
        <p>Profiles newly added dependencies. Catches phantom packages (< 7 days old), typosquatting via edit distance, Unicode homoglyph attacks, and circular staging by the same author.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 3 — Behavioral Fingerprinting</h3>
        <p>Diffs capability profiles between versions. Catches when a utility library suddenly gains network + process execution capabilities — the hallmark of a hijacked package.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Layer 4 — Registry Metadata</h3>
        <p>Analyzes publish history, maintainer account changes, rapid-fire releases, dormant package reactivation, and Sigstore provenance attestations.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

