<!-- FEATURES -->
<section class="vx-section" aria-labelledby="features-title">
  <div class="vx-wrap">
    <div class="section-label">Features</div>
    <h2 id="features-title">Beyond vulnerability databases</h2>
    <div class="vx-features-grid" role="list">
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Pre-Install Guard</h3>
        <p>Intercepts <code>npm install</code> and analyzes new packages before they execute. Diffs lockfiles, blocks critical threats, prompts on high signals, then runs the real install.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Verified Fix Recommendations</h3>
        <p>Every suggested upgrade version is cross-checked against OSV to ensure the fix itself isn't vulnerable. No circular fixes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>CI/CD Integration</h3>
        <p>GitHub Actions annotations, SARIF output for Advanced Security, and watch mode for continuous lockfile monitoring.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Fail Loud, Not Clean</h3>
        <p>If queries fail, vexes exits with code 2 and prints SCAN INCOMPLETE. A scanner that silently reports clean on failure is worse than useless.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Zero Dependencies</h3>
        <p>The dependency chain is the attack surface. vexes has none. Acorn parser is vendored. SQLite is Node.js built-in.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vx-feature-card" role="listitem">
        <h3>Undisableable Signals</h3>
        <p><code>KNOWN_COMPROMISED</code>, <code>PHANTOM_DEPENDENCY</code>, <code>CIRCULAR_STAGING</code>, and <code>CAPABILITY_ESCALATION</code> cannot be turned off via config.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

